## ginna: the container's init starts hwservicemanager before /dev/hwbinder is a
## usable binder node; it fails BINDER_SET_CONTEXT_MGR, and being `critical` it
## crash-loops until init gives up -> no hwbinder services -> no UI.
##
## Fix: mark it `disabled` + `override` and start it from `on post-fs`, after the
## binder nodes exist.
##
## /dev/vndbinder has the same race, but vndservicemanager is not `critical` so
## it can't be fixed the same way here: an early start that fails BINDER_SET_
## CONTEXT_MGR just sits there "running" without owning the node, and forcing it
## `disabled`+`override` and re-starting from post-fs makes init's rapid retries
## hit -EBUSY on the dead instance's binder_proc, so it never converges. Every
## handle-0 transaction on vndbinder then returns BR_DEAD_REPLY (kernel
## binder.c:3115), the graphics composer (ProcessState::initWithDriver(
## "/dev/vndbinder")) can't keep display.qservice alive, SDM HWCSession::Init()
## loops forever, IComposer/default never registers -> black screen.
##
## vndservicemanager is therefore left STOCK here (it ends up "running" but not
## owning /dev/vndbinder - a stable state) and recovered post-boot by
## /usr/libexec/halium-hal-recover: stop it, wait a real gap so the old
## binder_proc is released, start it clean, then restart the graphics composer.
## servicemanager (/dev/binder) comes up fine.

service hwservicemanager /system/bin/hwservicemanager
    class core
    user system
    group system readproc
    critical
    onrestart class_restart hal
    disabled
    override

on post-fs
    wait /dev/hwbinder 20
    wait /dev/binder 20
    start hwservicemanager
